// Exceptions: Throwing an exception is used to signal errors from functions, where "errors" are typically limited to only the following
//                  * Failures to meet the preconditions, such as failing to produce a valid return value object
//                  * Failures to meet the preconditions of another function that must be called.
//                  * (for non-private member functions) Failure to (re)establish a class invariant.
//             Instead of crashing, the program can:
//             1. Throw an exception when something goes wrong.
//             2. Catch it in a handler
//             3. and then recover gracefully.

// Why use Exceptions Handling: In any program errors happen - invalid input, missing files, failed memory allocation, ect
//                              Without exception handling, you have to check error manually after every operation. However,
//                              with exception handling the errors are checked automatically after each operation and can be
//                              handled appropriately and cleanly.

// What are Try-Catch blocks - Try catch blocks are around code operations that are "risky" - meaning they have the potential
//                             to cause errors such as I/O operations. Additionally, they allow you to handle error appropriate when they
//                             do occur.


// Exception Safety: Exception safety defines what guarantees your code provides if an exception is thrown.
//  * No-Throw Guarantee
//      - Function guarantees it will never throw an exception. Often marked noexcept.
//      - Example - Logging function, destructor, simple math
//  * Strong guarantee
//      - Operation either completes successfully or has no effect. If it fails, state is unchanged.
//      - Example: std::vector::push_back() - is uses a temporary before committing changes.
//  * Basic Guarantee
//      - State may change, but object remainders valid and usable (no leaks, corruption)
//      - Example: Most STL operations guarantee this.
//  * No Guarantee
//      - State may become invalid if exception occurs. Avoid this in well-designed code.
//      - Example: Poorly written custom code (no RAII, manual memory managment)

 Common C++ Exceptions

std::exception	Base class for all standard exceptions. Use it when you just want a general catch-all.	catch (const std::exception& e)
std::logic_error	Programmer mistake — invalid logic. (Shouldn’t happen in production if code is correct.)	Passing an invalid argument to a function
std::invalid_argument	Function received an invalid parameter.	sqrt(-1)
std::domain_error	Input value is outside valid mathematical domain.	log(-1)
std::length_error	Tried to create or resize a container beyond its capacity.	std::string s; s.resize(-1);
std::out_of_range	Accessed container element beyond bounds.	v.at(10) when v.size() == 5
std::runtime_error	Errors that only appear at runtime (I/O, file, network, etc.).	File read fails
std::overflow_error	Mathematical overflow occurs.	int x = INT_MAX + 1;
std::underflow_error	Mathematical underflow occurs.	Result too small to represent
std::bad_alloc	new fails due to memory exhaustion.	int* p = new int[999999999999];
std::bad_cast	dynamic_cast fails for references.	dynamic_cast<Derived&>(baseRef);
std::bad_typeid	Using typeid on null pointer.	typeid(*p) when p == nullptr
std::bad_exception	Unexpected exception type thrown in function with throw() specifier.	Legacy use (rare now)
